  {
    "episode_id": 50315,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50315.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "Well, Sam, thank you for that. Thank you for grounding us in all that we have been through this past summer. I represent all of Minneapolis, and I also represent 14 of the suburban municipalities surrounding Minneapolis as well as part of the Fifth Congressional District. And my father got diagnosed two days after George Floyd was murdered and passed away two weeks after that. And, you know, to simultaneously go through the the pain and the hurt and the unearthing of so many traumas that our community has experienced in the hands of the Minneapolis Police Department and systems that haven't adequately cared for us or included us and haven't really seen our full humanity and personhood. was painful. I didn't know George, but he was somebody's father. He was somebody's son, and he was a member of our community. And as I gone through my personal loss of losing one of the most important people in my life, because my father wasn't just a father, he was the only parent I ever knew. And past my teenage years, he'd become also my best friend. And it is, really hard to be of a community to experience the pain as part of that community, but then to also be in a position of influence and to be a policy maker, you're not limited, right? In the opportunities that you have in creating space for people to dialogue, in legislating people's cries. And so I've been on a journey really to honor that pain, honor that trauma, honor the hurt our community has experienced, and not just with the murder of George Floyd, but with losses of life due to a pandemic that has cost now the deaths of 200,000 people. And it's solely because of neglect, and people in leadership's inability to cohesively deal with the pandemic. Our journey has been to try to legislate Black lives, to have budgets that honor Black lives, to have policies when it comes to investment in healthcare, in education, in anti-policing, to center, right? That the reason we are mourning the loss of George Floyd, the reason we are mourning the loss of my father, the reason we're Seeing, you know, the pandemic disproportionately impact black and brown communities. The reason we are seeing police brutality disproportionately impact black and brown communities. All of that is. It's not an accident, right? It is because of decades and decades of policy decisions that people have made to neglect the economic and social needs of our communities. And so how do we undo that? We do that by creating policies that honor us and policies that allow for there to be proper investment in our humanity, in our dignity, in our personhood, in our opportunities, and in our joy.",
